- op: discuss  # 74a0
  at: '2026-03-30T15:43:41Z'  # 74a0
  by: agent  # 74a0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 74a0
  clock: 25  # 74a0
  nonce: 74a0  # 74a0
  message: "Investigated the 124 empty-path methods. The number likely comes from a bakeoff where route symbols were created without path metadata. Root causes vary by language:\n\n**Where empty paths are legitimate:** Django path('') and Flask route('/') represent valid root URL routes. Swift Vapor/Hummingbird handlers registered without explicit path inherit the group path. These should keep their route symbols — they represent real HTTP endpoints.\n\n**Where empty paths indicate extraction failures:** Some framework patterns produce handler symbols without resolvable paths:\n- Java Spring: @RequestMapping on a class (controller-level prefix) without method-level mapping — the handler exists but path requires combining class + method annotations\n- Express.js: app.use(middleware) registers a handler for all paths — no single path to extract\n- Go: http.HandleFunc with dynamically computed paths (variable, not string literal)\n\n**Would it be worthwhile to close this gap?**\n\nFor the extraction-failure cases (Spring class-level @RequestMapping, Express middleware): yes, these are architecturally significant handlers. Closing the gap requires:\n1. Spring: resolve class-level @RequestMapping prefix + method-level path (2-pass: collect class annotations, then combine with method annotations). Moderate effort, high value — Spring is extremely common.\n2. Express middleware: emit a route with path='*' or path='[middleware]' rather than empty string. Low effort, moderate value — makes middleware visible in route maps.\n3. Go dynamic paths: emit path='[dynamic]' marker. Low effort, low value — rare in practice.\n\nFor the legitimate-root-path cases: normalize to '/' consistently (Swift already does this, Django preserves ''). Trivial effort.\n\nTotal: ~2-3 days of work for Spring + Express + normalization. Spring alone would close the majority of the 124."  # 74a0
